ind of design and  nobody else is doing it so props to  Apple on that all right so we're all set  up and we're on the homescreen here and  I already made a few observations which  I'll share with you in just a second but  first impressions of the display  other than those bezels of course it  still looks quite good  now if I kind of look at it I could tell  that the colors aren't as saturated they  don't  as much so it looks a little bit more  faded a little bit mermoo did on the  colors in the contrast and they're just  looking at the icons themselves they  don't look as sharp I mean you can tell  I mean I love resolution we're using a  5k iMac a 5k LG ultra fine 4k projector  so I love high resolution so looking at  this at this distance even though they  say Retina display you can't tell at a  normal distance I mean I could tell now  does it look bad or does it suck it  definitely doesn't look sucky I mean  I've looked at a lot of different  Android LCD displays and some of them  really look terrible even if they have  higher resolutions like even the 2.7 K  on an LG that I had the colors sucked  the brightness sucked even though it's  high-res it was not a great screen so I  want to go ahead and max out this  brightness here and it does get quite  bright so this is the first thing I want  to compare against the iPhone 10 so here  we are in Amazon's home screen and the  10s display the OLED is definitely  brighter we have more contrast deeper  blacks it does look better this does  definitely look like a you know a few  generations behind so let's shift it to  the side and yeah I mean we definitely  get noticeably darker kind of brighten a  shift when we go from the side where the  OLED stays almost as bright so it is an  inferior display now Apple says this is  their best LCD display so we have the  previous latest phone the iPhone 8 plus  here turned off true tone because the  color differences were apparent in  looking straight at the phones the  iPhone 10 R is brighter but interesting  Vadim over there is saying that this  screen on the iPhone 10 are off axis is  not as bright and I could see the same  thing now where the off axis performance  isn't as good maybe that has something  to do with the backlight with all these  curved edges and stuff where they can't  hide it as well so before we take a look  at some 8k footage on YouTube and  compare it to the 10s and 10's max I  want to show you something that just  jumped out to me I was trying to use a  force touch here and pressing down and  it just wouldn't go in here I was  pushing down hard whereas with the  iPhone 8 plus you guys can see you kind  of go and instantly just do a really  hard press you're in here you do a hard  press it's not doing anything so you  actually have to hold down for  or maybe about a second or so and then  you kind of get into the menu so that's  definitely something you might have to  adjust to so now let's go ahead and open  up YouTube here I have Japan and 8k  opened up and you can't actually watch  8k on your iPhone on none of these even  the ones that are higher resolution the  max you can do is 1080p so with iPhone  10s and 10's max it actually takes 1080p  and has to stretch it out a bit to fill  all the pixels on the screen whereas  with the iPhone 10 R it actually down  samples it and to the display we have  true tone on on both and the iPhone 10s  is warmer interestingly and then if you  look at off access the OLED screens  actually get a little bit bluer the the  color temperature changes whereas the  LCD doesn't the white balance stays the  same but our exposure it gets a little  darker off axis so yeah we're a little  bit sharper on the 10s but definitely  not as much of a difference as on the  home screen so if you look at the home  screen side-by-side you definitely see  the icons are much more detailed  especially we try looking up closer but  here where we're pulling 1080p on both  and down sampling it on the 10r and then  